Description: antinomie: record where reads and writes of files happened, and what in-kernel copies moved
 The host rebuilds a file from the calls that wrote it, and so far it
 placed each write at the offset it inferred from opens, lseek and
 O_APPEND. That inference breaks where the kernel alone knows the
 position: a descriptor shared after fork, dup or SCM_RIGHTS moves for
 every task that uses it, and an O_APPEND write lands at the end of the
 file as it is when the write takes the inode lock. In-kernel copies
 (sendfile, splice, copy_file_range, reflink clones) and fallocate
 change a file with no bytes in any argument at all.
 .
 Two kernel-imported fields of a call's exit say what happened, for
 seekable files (regular files and block devices) and calls that
 succeeded:
 .
 - file.position (antinomie.filepos/1, 24 bytes): where a read, write,
   pread64, pwrite64, readv, writev, preadv, pwritev, preadv2 or
   pwritev2 began in the file and how many bytes it moved, from the
   position the file system left behind, and the write order.
 - file.copy (antinomie.filecopy/1, 104 bytes): the kind of copy, the
   source and destination files (device, inode, generation, mode), where
   the range began in each, its length, the destination's size after the
   call, and for fallocate its mode. When the destination is seekable
   and the source is not (the pipe of a splice: sendfile and
   copy_file_range need a seekable source), the bytes cannot be read
   again later, so the bytes the destination got follow as
   file.copy.data, at most 1 MiB per call; the record says when more
   went in. A seekable source is not carried: the host
   rebuilds the bytes from what it knows of that file.
 .
 Two processes that write one range at the same time are applied one
 after the other under the inode lock, but their exits can be recorded
 in the other order: the position hooks run after the lock is dropped,
 and the exit is stamped later still. Each write therefore takes a
 number from one guest-wide counter in generic_write_checks_count(),
 which the file systems run holding the inode lock (the write order, in
 both records), so the host replays overlapping writes in the order the
 file system applied them.
 .
 The hooks record into the task's recorder context during the call and
 the exit publishes the fields, so a call costs no extra event. Only the
 system call itself is recorded; the hooked functions are shared with
 io_uring and exec, which the call number tells apart.
 .
 Hooked kernel files:
 - fs/read_write.c: vfs_read(), vfs_write(), vfs_readv() and
   vfs_writev(), where every read and write system call ends with the
   position its transfer left; do_sendfile() and copy_file_range(),
   which hold both files and both positions.
 - fs/splice.c: do_splice(), the file side of a splice;
   iter_file_splice_write(), where the bytes from a pipe reach the
   destination file while the pipe still holds them.
 - fs/ioctl.c: ioctl_file_clone(), shared by FICLONE and FICLONERANGE.
 - fs/open.c: vfs_fallocate(), shared by fallocate and the
   preallocation ioctls.
 .
